+**Pointer position on click** *(experimental, unreleased; [docs/rfc-pointer-snapshot.md](docs/rfc-pointer-snapshot.md))*. `set(--x, mouse(x))` stores the event's `clientX` in px; `mouse(x, local)` measures from the border box of the element with the `on:` declaration, as the live `mouse(x, local)` does. It is a snapshot: the state does not follow the pointer afterwards. Only in `click(…)`, `pointerenter(…)`, `pointerleave(…)` and `outside(click | pointerdown, …)`; elsewhere it is a compile error. A click without a pointer (Enter or Space, `element.click()`) gives the center of that element. The state must be a length state, such as `state(0px)`. `set(--x, var(--y))` is still not supported.
+
+```css
+.area { --menu-x: state(0px); --menu-y: state(0px);
+  on: click(set(--menu-x, mouse(x, local)), set(--menu-y, mouse(y, local)), set(--open, true)); }
+```
+
 **Order and timing.** Actions run synchronously, in order, while the event is dispatched; each one reads the value the previous one wrote (50 synchronous increments give 50). Since 0.5.1, before an event is dispatched, the logical consequences of earlier actions in the same frame (state values, `:when()` and `:nth-var()` markers) are applied, so conditional `on:` rules always see the current state, even for several clicks before the next frame. Visual updates still happen in the next frame. Events not on the list above (`submit`, `animationstart`, `keyup`…) are compile errors. Actions never stop propagation.
 
